  "brief": "**Idea.** Tune a publication instead of moving through a menu.\n\n**Composition.** An enormous amber instrument dial occupies the page, with radial station labels, fine calibration ticks and the programme title nested at its centre.\n\n**Navigation and complete example.** Tune six fictional late-night programmes, read their transcripts and liner notes, and save an ordered listening sequence. This silent specimen demonstrates tuning without a live radio dependency.\n\n**Responsive and inclusive behaviour.** The dial scales to the phone width. A labelled range and next/previous controls provide precise selection independent of spatial pointing. All controls are native buttons or labelled inputs. Movement is discretionary, no sound starts automatically, reduced-motion preferences remove transitions, and a direct index exposes the same content.\n\n**Adaptation.** Use meaningful frequency stops and programme content inside the instrument. Keep listening optional and offer complete transcripts. Retain the relationship between navigation and content. Do not reduce the reference to a generic hero followed by interchangeable cards.\n\n**Research.** [Radio Garden — About](https://radio.garden/settings/radio-garden); [Typatone](https://typatone.com/). These are conceptual precedents, not copied artwork, source code, branding or text. All diagrams and sample writing in this specimen are original, fictional material.",
  "scope": "Complete visual reference with deterministic local interactions and fictional content; no production backend."
